{"id": "2026-11-16-news-estonia-to-shut-russia-border-crossings", "date": "2026-11-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Estonia to shut Russia border crossings", "summary": "Estonia will close its road border crossings with Russia on 1 December after a GRU-attributed arson attempt on drone maker Threod Systems.", "url": "https://news.err.ee/1610196794/estonia-to-close-border-crossings-with-russia-after-gru-linked-sabotage-attacks", "source_domain": "news.err.ee", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "security"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "Estonia will close its road border crossings with Russia on Tuesday 1 December, its foreign ministry said on Sunday 15 November. The move follows an attempted arson attack on a drone maker near Tallinn that Estonia's Internal Security Service (KAPO) attributes to Russia's military intelligence service, the GRU. Eight Latvian citizens were detained after trying to break into the premises of Threod Systems in Viimsi on Saturday morning, 14 November, with the aim of causing an explosion and a fire, according to the public broadcaster ERR. Threod makes long-range reconnaissance and attack drones. This is the second attempted arson attack on an Estonian defence firm since August, when Milrem Robotics was targeted. Prime Minister Kristen Michal called it \"part of Russia's broader hybrid war against Europe\", and Foreign Minister Margus Tsahkna said closing the crossings would also make it harder to get around sanctions. The three crossings affected, Narva, Luhamaa and Koidula, are among the last land routes into Russia still open to travellers from the EU. Russian foreign ministry spokeswoman Maria Zakharova said that, if confirmed, the plan would be \"unbridled Russophobia\". The attribution to the GRU comes from Estonian authorities and has not been independently verified.", "why": "an EU and NATO state is shutting a land border in response to sabotage against its arms industry. It is the clearest step yet by a Baltic state from naming Russia's hybrid attacks to imposing a concrete cost for them.", "thread_id": "2026-11-16-news-estonia-to-shut-russia-border-crossings", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-16", "event_date": "2026-11-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Estonia", "Russia", "GRU", "Threod Systems", "Narva"]}
{"id": "2026-11-16-news-prague-s-biggest-protest-since-1989", "date": "2026-11-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Prague's biggest protest since 1989", "summary": "Hundreds of thousands rallied in Prague against a bill scrapping the licence fee for Czech public broadcasters.", "url": "https://www.lemonde.fr/international/article/2026/11/16/a-prague-400-000-manifestants-denoncent-une-reforme-de-l-audiovisuel-public-tcheque_6811567_3210.html", "source_domain": "lemonde.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Hundreds of thousands of people rallied in Prague's Letná park on Sunday 15 November against the government's plan to scrap the licence fee that funds Czech public television and radio. Organisers put the crowd at 400,000, and police, while giving no figure, said it was the largest demonstration since the fall of communism in 1989. The bill, which passed its first reading on Saturday 24 October, would replace the fee with money from the state budget and cut public broadcasters' budgets by 15 to 20%, according to Le Monde. Critics, including the European Broadcasting Union, the European Federation of Journalists and Reporters Without Borders, say it would put Czech Television and Czech Radio under the control of the government. Prime Minister Andrej Babiš, whose ANO party governs with the SPD and the Motorists, says his government does not want to control the public media and that \"none of what the people are protesting against… is true\". The protest was organised by the Million Moments for Democracy group.", "why": "moving public broadcasters from a dedicated fee onto the yearly state budget makes their funding a decision for whichever government is in office. That is the mechanism media-freedom groups warn about, and the size of the protest shows how contested it is.", "thread_id": "2026-11-16-news-prague-s-biggest-protest-since-1989", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-16", "event_date": "2026-11-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Czech Republic", "Andrej Babiš", "Czech Television", "ANO"]}
{"id": "2026-11-16-news-third-us-carrier-reaches-middle-east", "date": "2026-11-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Third US carrier reaches Middle East", "summary": "The USS Theodore Roosevelt arrived in the CENTCOM area, joining two other US carriers as US-Iran talks stall.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/16/uss-roosevelt-arrives-in-middle-east-is-the-us-iran-war-about-to-resume", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The aircraft carrier USS Theodore Roosevelt has arrived in the Middle East, a US official told Al Jazeera on Monday 16 November. It is the third US carrier now in the area run by US Central Command (CENTCOM). The deployment comes as talks to turn the US–Iran truce into a lasting settlement and to reopen the Strait of Hormuz remain stalled. Pakistan and Qatar are mediating. Last week, President Donald Trump said further strikes on Iran were \"quite unlikely\" and the military part of the war \"effectively over\", while saying US forces remained \"locked and loaded\". The Revolutionary Guards claimed drone attacks on a US site in Bahrain on Friday 13 and Sunday 15 November. The Roosevelt left San Diego in September, so its arrival had been expected.", "why": "Trump has called the war \"effectively over\", but a third carrier adds strike capacity and points the other way, and the president's own statements have not been a reliable guide to what happens next.", "thread_id": "2026-11-16-news-third-us-carrier-reaches-middle-east", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-16", "event_date": "2026-11-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["United States", "Iran", "USS Theodore Roosevelt", "CENTCOM", "Strait of Hormuz"]}
{"id": "2026-11-16-news-icc-judges-close-us-afghanistan-probe", "date": "2026-11-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "ICC judges close US Afghanistan probe", "summary": "ICC judges closed the investigation into alleged torture by US forces in Afghanistan and CIA sites, citing evidentiary limits and delay.", "url": "https://www.lemonde.fr/international/article/2026/11/16/cpi-des-juges-referment-le-dossier-sur-les-crimes-des-forces-americaines-en-afghanistan_6811690_3210.html", "source_domain": "lemonde.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Three judges of the International Criminal Court (ICC) closed its investigation into alleged crimes by US forces in Afghanistan on Thursday 12 November, Le Monde reported on Monday 16 November. The investigation, opened in 2020, covered torture, rape and sexual violence at the US base at Bagram, north of Kabul, in 2003 and 2004. It also covered CIA secret prisons in Romania, Poland and Lithuania. According to Le Monde, the judges cited \"significant limits\" on the prosecution's ability to investigate and gather evidence, and said it was now too late to bring charges. Lawyers for former detainees at the US Center for Constitutional Rights had argued that the evidence of a torture policy, including the US Senate's own report, was overwhelming. Le Monde says Washington had made closing the case one of its conditions for not sanctioning the Court ahead of April 2027.", "why": "this was one of the few avenues for international accountability for US abuses after 9/11. It is closing at a moment when the US is pressuring the Court. The judges, for their part, gave procedural reasons: the time that has passed and the limits on gathering evidence.", "thread_id": "2026-11-16-news-icc-judges-close-us-afghanistan-probe", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-16", "event_date": "2026-11-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["International Criminal Court", "United States", "Afghanistan", "Bagram", "CIA"]}
{"id": "2026-11-16-news-neves-re-elected-in-cape-verde", "date": "2026-11-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Neves re-elected in Cape Verde", "summary": "José Maria Neves won re-election with 63.8% in the first round, per provisional results.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/cape-verde-election-jose-maria-neves-set-to-win-second-term/a-79952606", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Cape Verde's President José Maria Neves won a second five-year term in the first round on Sunday 15 November, with 63.8% of the vote according to the electoral commission's provisional results. His main challenger, former justice minister Joana Rosa Amado of the Movement for Democracy (MpD), took about 31.6%. Four other candidates shared the rest. Le Temps, citing AFP, says turnout was low. Neves promised to keep \"promoting freedoms\" in the archipelago, which ranks third in Africa on the Mo Ibrahim Foundation's 2026 governance index. Observers from ECOWAS (the West African regional bloc), the African Union and the EU monitored the vote.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-16-news-neves-re-elected-in-cape-verde", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-16", "event_date": "2026-11-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Cape Verde", "José Maria Neves", "Joana Rosa Amado"]}
{"id": "2026-11-16-news-swiss-economy-grows-0-2-in-third-quarter", "date": "2026-11-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss economy grows 0.2% in third quarter", "summary": "Seco's first estimate puts Q3 2026 Swiss GDP growth at 0.2%, led by industry with services flat.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/economie/le-pib-suisse-a-augmente-de-0-2-au-troisieme-trimestre", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Switzerland's real GDP grew 0.2% in the third quarter of 2026, according to the first estimate from the State Secretariat for Economic Affairs (Seco), published on Monday 16 November. Industry grew again while services as a whole stagnated, the wire agency ATS reports. The growth is well below the second quarter, when GDP rose 1.5%, according to the same report. Economists polled by the financial news agency AWP had expected anywhere from −0.3% to +0.4%, so the result is in the middle of that range. The full Seco release, with the breakdown by sector and the figure adjusted for major sporting events, was not reachable this run.", "why": "if services, the larger part of the economy, are flat while industry carries growth, the slowdown is broad. That feeds into cantonal budget forecasts and the Swiss National Bank's next rate decision.", "thread_id": "2026-11-16-news-swiss-economy-grows-0-2-in-third-quarter", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-16", "event_date": "2026-11-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Seco", "Switzerland"]}
{"id": "2026-11-16-news-swiss-asylum-requests-fall-16", "date": "2026-11-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss asylum requests fall 16%", "summary": "SEM registered 2,210 asylum applications in October 2026, 16% fewer than a year earlier.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/W11FHTquJJ9Z", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Switzerland registered 2,210 asylum applications in October 2026, 16% fewer than in October 2025 (433 fewer), the State Secretariat for Migration (SEM) said on Monday 16 November. Afghanistan remained the main country of origin with 498 applications, followed by Eritrea (430), Algeria (236), Ethiopia (171) and Somalia (163). Applications rose by 88 (4%) from September, which the SEM says is the usual late-summer and autumn rise before winter. The SEM decided 2,419 cases at first instance and granted asylum to 567 people. A total of 732 people left Switzerland under official supervision: 418 on their own and 314 under police escort, either to their home country, to the EU state responsible under the Dublin rules, or to another country.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-16-news-swiss-asylum-requests-fall-16", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-16", "event_date": "2026-11-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["SEM", "Switzerland", "Afghanistan", "Eritrea"]}
